WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:10.389
LEAKED SSNS:
Have you ever heard of a company called Equifax?

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:14.040
You might’ve seen their logo when applying
for a new credit card or taking out a car

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.260
loan or mortgage.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:20.019
They’re one of the three largest credit
bureaus in the world alongside Experian and

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:24.500
TransUnion, and they’re in charge of determining
your credit worthiness.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.720
Given their role in the market, I don’t
think you’d be surprised to hear that Equifax

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.530
deals with a significant amount of sensitive
data like name, age, date of birth, address,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.210
phone number, annual income, and social security
number.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:43.760
This has made Equifax a prime target for hackers
and cyberterrorists looking to commit identity

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.450
theft and credit card fraud.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:50.260
Now, I’m sure that Equifax trys their best
to protect this information give that their

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:55.649
company’s reputation is on the line, but
unfortunately, their efforts weren’t quite

00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:56.649
an enough.

00:00:56.649 --> 00:01:01.870
In September of 2017, Equifax announced a
ground breaking data breach that affected

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:06.689
a mind boggling 147 million people.

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:11.310
And given the nature of their business, the
data being leaked wasn’t your cat pictures

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.800
or youtube subscriptions, it was your financial
profile.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.190
It was the names of a 147 million people,
the date of births of a 147 million people,

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:25.689
the social security numbers of 146 million
people, and so much more.

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:30.250
This data breach was so large that if you’re
an American and watching this video, it’s

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.650
more likely that you were affected than it
is that you were not affected.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:39.909
Here’s the thing, the US population stands
at 330 million people out of which 22.3% are

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:41.380
under 18.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.479
This means that only 256 million Americans
were even capable of having data saved Equifax.

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:52.460
And yes, I know that teenagers under 18 can
have a credit card, but that’s extremely

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:53.460
rare.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.990
So, out of this 256 million Americans, 146
million had their SSNs leaked.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:04.549
In other words, if you were an American adult
in 2017 and had any sort of credit history,

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:09.380
there’s over a 55% chance that your SSN
was leaked through this data breach.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.230
And even if yours wasn’t leaked, it’s
highly likely that you know someone who’s

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.110
was, and that’s just in America.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.800
This data breech also affected 15.2 million
people in the UK and 19,000 Canadians.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.730
But, how did such a catastrophic data breach
even materialize and what can you do if you

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.370
were affected?

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:37.760
DATA BREACH:
Taking a look back, the story of this data

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.550
breach dates back to December of 2016, months
before the breach took place.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:48.190
At the time, a security researcher looked
into the servers and websites of Equifax,

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:50.490
and he noticed something strange.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:55.510
It appeared that everyday people had access
to some sort of employee site, and you didn’t

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.670
even need credentials to login.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.420
One of the portals on this site had search
fields and you could search through the personal

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.330
data of Equifax customers.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:08.090
"All you had to do was put in a search term
and get millions of results, just instantly.”

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.380
And that was just the beginning of it.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:16.799
The researcher also found that it was extraordinarily
easy to get shell access to several Equifax

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:17.799
servers.

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:22.099
Additionally, they also uncovered multiple
vulnerabilities such as SQL injection.

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:27.440
While these were all major security risks,
on the brightside, they were also very easy

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.440
to patch.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.730
They could’ve taken down the employee portal
in minutes, and they could’ve patched the

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.390
server flaws within a week.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.340
And Equifax even had the opportunity to do
exactly this given that the researcher reached

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.269
out to them and made the vulnerabilities clear.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:45.519
But, as you would guess, Equifax didn’t
do that atleast not quick enough according

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:47.100
to the researcher.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.560
This security researcher wasn’t the only
one that was tipping off Equifax either.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.620
A few months later in March of 2017, the US
Department of Homeland Security would reach

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.930
out to Equifax regarding the need to patch
some of their software.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.739
Given that this notice was literally coming
for the US government and not some security

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:06.110
researcher, Equifax would immediately take
action.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.810
Just one day following the notice, Equifax
would notify their cybersecurity personel

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:14.079
about the need upgrade their Apache Struts
software.

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:17.920
Within just 48 hours, they would apparently
patch the bugs.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.240
And about a week later, the security department
would even run scans to verify that the bugs

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.100
were patched, and the scans apparently revealed
no vulnerabilities.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:31.420
But for some reason, none of these efforts
actually patched the bugs.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:36.220
To be honest, it appears to me that Equifax
cared more about putting down on paper that

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.830
they responded quickly to the government notice
than they cared about actually fixing the

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:40.830
bug.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.850
But, that’s just my own speculation.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:49.139
Anyway, regardless of why the bug wasn’t
patched, the bottomline is that it wasn’t

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:54.009
patched, and this opened up the perfect opportunity
for hackers to strike.

00:04:54.009 --> 00:04:58.880
About 2 months later on May 13, 2017, attackers
would begin accessing sensitive personal data

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.400
using this bug.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.620
And it wasn’t just a one time thing either.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.800
They would consistently access sensitive data
for the next 2 and half months.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.630
And it wasn’t till July 29 that Equifax’s
security department would finally notice suspicious

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.590
network traffic.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.380
Equifax would of course patch the bug for
real this time almost instantaneously, but

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:21.500
the damage had already been done.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:28.060
PUBLIC BACKLASH:
Initially, Equifax had no idea about the magnitude

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.840
or depth of the data breach.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.470
In the words of the CEO himself “I certainly
did not know that personal identifying information

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:39.050
('PII') had been stolen, or have any indication
of the scope of this attack.”

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:44.590
Nonetheless, Equifax would go ahead and get
their lawyers on board and follow their data

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:45.850
breach protocol.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:50.729
One of the first steps in this protocol was
to complete a thorough forensic investigation.

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:55.350
And let’s just say that it didn’t take
long for this investigation to reveal some

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.660
terrible news.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:02.100
By August 11, it was clear to Equifax that
this wasn’t just some small leak.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.630
It appeared that the hackers had accessed
entire databases.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:10.780
And the data being stolen was spread across
a multitude of datatables which made it especially

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:14.100
hard to link the stolen data with individual
customers.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:18.700
This is one of the main reasons that it took
Equifax so long to publicly announce the data

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:19.700
breech.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:24.530
But eventually, by September 4, the investigative
team would put together a comprehensive list

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.260
of 143 million victims.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:33.210
And on September 7, 2017, Equifax would publicly
announce exactly what happened.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:36.710
As you would guess, the reaction was not pretty.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:40.750
Equifax stock would crater nearly 40% within
a matter of days.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:45.220
And the public would become more aware of
the company than ever before.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:50.060
Interest in Equifax literally exploded 20X
in the days following the public announcement.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.440
But, almost as quickly as the drama unfolded,
the drama wrapped up.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:00.680
By the end of 2017, interest in Equifax returned
to normal nonexistent levels.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:05.000
The stock took longer to recover, but that
too would return to all time highs within

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.000
2 years.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.580
In fact, since then, Equifax stock has grown
as much as 102%.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:15.440
Somehow, Equifax has been able to live this
down with extremely low levels of backlash

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.139
from the general public.

00:07:17.139 --> 00:07:21.920
And I think the main reason for this is that
the average person didn’t have strong opinions

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.680
about Equifax going into the drama.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:30.120
Imagine if this same thing happened with Facebook
who people already associate with data intrusion.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.460
We’d literally have riots on social media
even though the data leaked by Facebook would

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.440
probably be less sensitive.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.810
This isn’t to say that Equifax didn’t
receive any backlash at all per se.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:45.300
However, the backlash that they did receive
was rather short lived and people forgot about

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.800
it pretty quickly.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.821
And given that the public wasn’t really
holding Equifax accountable, it was up to

00:07:50.821 --> 00:07:53.550
Equifax themselves and regulators to take
action.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:58.090
Much of Equifax’s leadership would either
leave voluntarily or be forced out.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:02.710
Equifax’s Chief Information Officer, Chief
Security Officer, and CEO would all retire

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.379
within a few weeks of the public announcement.

00:08:05.379 --> 00:08:08.449
It appears that these guys preferred to take
the easy way out.

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:14.060
In terms of the FTC, they would eventually
settle with Equifax for $575 million to $700

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.350
million depending on depending on how many
people claimed damages.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:22.370
While this is definitely a hefty fine for
government standards, it’s not too bad from

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.330
Equifax’s perspective.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.659
Afterall, Equifax makes more than that on
an annual basis.

00:08:27.659 --> 00:08:33.360
So, they were only set back 1 year even though
they leaked the data of literally half of

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.360
American adults.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:39.870
In the end, Equifax was able to get away with
a small slap on the wrist, but the same could

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.409
not be said about the victims.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:48.130
THE FALLOUT:
Ironically, one of the first problems that

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:51.149
victims faced was due to Equifax once again.

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:56.220
You see, shortly after the breach became public,
Equifax created a website where people could

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.769
claim losses.

00:08:57.769 --> 00:09:03.580
This was definitely the right thing to do,
but unfortunately, Equifax had missteps here

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:04.580
as well.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:09.740
Instead of creating a portal under their official
domain, Equifax.com, Equifax decided that

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:16.339
it would be a great idea to create a new domain
just for claims called equifaxsecurity2017.com.

00:09:16.339 --> 00:09:20.350
To this day, I have no idea why they decided
to do this.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:25.700
Maybe it was to distance the claims from their
main website, I’m not sure, but what I am

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:29.680
sure about it is that this new domain was
a terrible decision.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.350
First of all, it made it hard for people to
tell whether they were on the official website

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.350
or not.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:38.079
And this led to the rise of several scam websites
with similar domains.

00:09:38.079 --> 00:09:43.590
Even Equifax’s own support team was confused,
and at one point, they were directing consumers

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.589
to one of these fake websites themselves.

00:09:46.589 --> 00:09:51.579
This is clearly not a great look, but on the
brightside, correctly claiming damages with

00:09:51.579 --> 00:09:54.709
Equifax could result in a payout as high as
$20,000.

00:09:54.709 --> 00:09:58.529
This initial claim window closed up at the
beginning of 2020.

00:09:58.529 --> 00:10:02.830
But an extended claim window is still open
until January of 2024.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:08.690
The average victim will obviously not be paid
anywhere near $20,000, but if you had any

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.930
sort of credit file in 2017 or before, it’s
worth checking it out.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.690
You may be able to get a few hundred dollars
from them.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:20.570
This compensation is of course not worth having
your data compromised, but there’s also

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.860
no reason to forgo claiming this compensation.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:27.850
Also, you’ll probably get a much better
idea as to how much data was stolen from you

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:28.930
if any.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:33.459
Aside from offering claims, Equifax also rolled
out a couple of goodwill programs shortly

00:10:33.459 --> 00:10:35.089
after the fiasco.

00:10:35.089 --> 00:10:39.490
For example, they offered victims free credit
monitoring services for four years.

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:45.180
Now, you might be saying, why would you want
to give Equifax anymore data given their history,

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.829
and that is a fair point.

00:10:46.829 --> 00:10:50.430
But, you can look at it from a different perspective
as well.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.060
Given what happened to Equifax, it’s likely
that they’re more keen on cybersecurity

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:55.459
than ever before.

00:10:55.459 --> 00:11:01.060
They’ve apparently invested $1.5 billion
into technology and security since 2018.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:07.210
So, you would hope that things are different,
but then again, Experian and TransUnion probably

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:14.990
also buffed up their security after this incident,
so it may be better to just stick with them

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:15.990
instead.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:19.519
THE TAKEAWAY:
If we learned anything from this incident,

00:11:19.519 --> 00:11:24.670
it’s that the biggest threats to our privacy
may not be as straightforward as Google or

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:26.079
Facebook.

00:11:26.079 --> 00:11:30.100
Background financial companies like credit
bureaus, lenders, and underwriters have more

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.200
sensitive data about all of us than big tech
does.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.880
Yet, they receive virtually no criticism because
we don’t interact with these companies on

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:43.089
a regular basis and them collecting this data
is seen as an essential part of their business.

00:11:43.089 --> 00:11:48.030
But, the only way that we can get these institutions
to take on more accountability is by shining

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.279
the spotlight on them just like we have been
doing with big tech.

00:11:51.279 --> 00:11:56.040
Apple’s “ask not to track” feature was
a gamechanger, and the main reason that came

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:00.300
to fruition is thanks to all the attention
surrounding big tech data collection.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.970
If there was more attention surrounding the
credit space, consumers may get more choice

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:05.600
there as well.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:10.180
For example, when we apply for a credit with
Amex, we might be able to choose which credit

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:12.010
bureau they use to run our credit.

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:17.040
By implementing such features, we may able
to prevent future instances of complacency

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.040
from occuring.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:19.860
But, that’s just what I think.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.370
Did you know about this massive data leak
from Equifax?

